# Use Types Over Interfaces

Always use TypeScript `type` instead of `interface` for better consistency and flexibility.

Key points:
- Use `type` for all type definitions
- Use `type` for ports/contracts
- Use `type` for domain models
- Use `type` for DTOs and request/response types

Example:
```typescript
// ✅ Correct
type Movie = {
  id: number;
  title: string;
};

type MovieRepository = {
  getMovies(): Promise<Movie[]>;
};

// ❌ Incorrect
interface Movie {
  id: number;
  title: string;
}

interface MovieRepository {
  getMovies(): Promise<Movie[]>;
}
```

This applies to all TypeScript files including:
- [src/core/domain/models/Movie.ts](mdc:src/core/domain/models/Movie.ts)
- [src/core/ports/MovieRepository.ts](mdc:src/core/ports/MovieRepository.ts)

Rationale:
- Types are more flexible and support unions/intersections better
- Types provide better error messages
- Types are more consistent with functional programming principles
- Types prevent accidental interface merging
